+## 198 - (Fixed/Improved) Likely Base AdvCiv Worker/land-development underperformance, compounded by an AdvCiv-SAS Worker-demand regression and too-low minimum, could leave mature AI cities badly unimproved
+
+Primary empirical reproduction and validation in AdvCiv-SAS: save file 459.
+
+Historical evidence suggests that the broader symptom predates AdvCiv-SAS, although not necessarily with the same code-level cause. In the [this CFC forum post](https://forums.civfanatics.com/threads/ai-city-placement-and-misc-suggestions.695343/page-7#post-16782814), Base AdvCiv example 208 problem 1 records that "Shanghai city is almost not improved at all in 325 AD despite being founded in 2320 BC". The same report categorizes examples 190+ under what it calls AdvCiv 1.12.3. That older observation does not prove that Base AdvCiv had the exact later AdvCiv-SAS regression described below, but it does show that severe long-lived Worker/land-development underperformance was already a high-priority Base AdvCiv concern before the SAS Worker refactors.
+
+Save file 459 exposed the same class of problem very clearly in AdvCiv-SAS. Russia had only 3 land Workers by turn 100 and still only 3 on turn 220 despite having 6 cities and very large populations. On turn 150, 39 of 62 currently worked plots were unimproved and Moscow alone worked 8 unimproved tiles; by turn 200, 58 of 91 worked plots were unimproved and Moscow worked 9 unimproved tiles. This initially looked like a Worker movement failure, but the Workers that did exist were actively building useful improvements, and once more Workers were finally produced after turn 220 the empire improved rapidly. The immediate bottleneck was therefore Worker availability/demand rather than the core `AI_bestCityBuild` execution logic.
+
+One AdvCiv-SAS-specific cause was an accounting regression created by an otherwise intentional Worker refactor. Ordinary land improvement selection had been moved out of the legacy city best-build valuation and into the custom `CvUnitAI::AI_bestCityBuild` logic so SAS could control Cottage/Farm/Mine/etc. choice without vanilla city valuation interfering. However, `CvCityAI::AI_updateWorkersHaveAndNeeded` still used `AI_getBestBuild(ePlot) != NO_BUILD` to decide whether an unimproved city tile represented Worker demand. For ordinary featureless land, that legacy cache can now deliberately be `NO_BUILD` even though a SAS Worker has a valid and useful improvement to make. A worked Grassland tile could therefore be visible to Worker movement as useful work while simultaneously being invisible to the city Worker-demand calculation.
+
+To fix that mismatch without creating a second improvement-selection system, currently worked, featureless owned land now counts as Worker demand even when the legacy city best-build cache is empty. The exact improvement remains entirely controlled by `AI_bestCityBuild`; there is no duplicated terrain/feature/bonus valuation and no hardcoded Cottage/Farm/Mine choice. Unworked plots retain the stricter inherited best-build check, which is important for KI#192: completed secondary areas can still correctly fall to zero Worker demand instead of creating speculative Workers with no actual job.
+
+That regression fix was meaningful but exposed a separate and broader weakness in Worker policy. In the first corrected save-file 459 rerun, Russia improved from 39 to 26 unimproved worked plots on turn 150 and from 58 to 24 on turn 200, but it still had only 7 Workers for 6 cities on turn 200. Inspection showed that this was not another failed production gate: the old AdvCiv-SAS guaranteed primary-area minimum was effectively only 1 Worker per city plus 1 reserve for the capital area. The larger approximately 2.5 Workers-per-city formula elsewhere in Base AdvCiv was only a maximum production cap (and decayed by era), not a target or minimum. Russia reaching exactly 7 Workers for 6 primary-area cities therefore meant the old floor was being enforced; the floor itself was simply much too conservative for the importance of land development.
+
+The primary-area standing minimum is therefore now shared and XML-tunable through `SAS_AI_WORKER_PRIMARY_AREA_MINIMUM_PERCENT_PER_CITY` and `SAS_AI_WORKER_PRIMARY_AREA_MINIMUM_RESERVE`, defaulting to 200% per city plus 1 reserve (e.g. 5 cities -> 11 Workers, 6 cities -> 13 Workers). `AI_neededWorkers`, the relevant production ceilings and Worker-scrapping safeguards all respect the same minimum so one subsystem does not immediately undo another. The existing one-city early-growth protection remains: a tiny first capital is not allowed to chain-build the full multi-Worker floor before it has grown enough. Secondary areas also remain demand-driven rather than inheriting the primary-area standing force.
+
+The final save-file 459 validation showed a much stronger development pattern. Russia had 9 Workers for 4 cities by turn 100 and 11 Workers for 5 cities by turn 150. At turn 150, 47 of 54 worked plots were improved (only 7 unimproved), and Moscow had all 16 currently worked plots improved. By turn 200, Russia still had 11 Workers for 5 cities and 61 of 79 worked plots were improved, with Moscow at 17 of 19. The autoplay branches naturally diverged after the behavior change, so these are diagnostic comparisons rather than identical-game snapshots, but the Worker-count floor itself is directly visible and the reduction in under-improved worked land is large.
+
+This is therefore both a regression fix and a deliberate AI-strength enhancement, while also addressing a broader symptom that appears to have existed in Base AdvCiv already. Workers convert population and territory into food, production, commerce, routes and cleared terrain, so chronically running near one Worker per city can leave otherwise strong AI empires unable to exploit their own land. Further reserve/ratio tuning can still be revisited empirically, but the previous pathological shortage is considered fixed/improved for now.
+
+Fixed/improved with the help of GPT-5.6 Thinking thanks.
+
+## 199 - (Fixed/Improved) Base AdvCiv/K-Mod generic UnitAI fallback could use dedicated land anti-air units as ordinary attackers or reserves
+
+Primary empirical reproduction and validation: save file 459.
+
+Save file 459 exposed a long-standing late-game composition problem in which dedicated land anti-air units could occupy a very large share of an AI army. In one run, Saladin had 108 combat units on turn 350, including 41 Mobile SAMs. BBAI production traces showed that this was not only legitimate anti-air demand: Mobile SAM was repeatedly selected for generic `UNITAI_ATTACK`, `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY`, and `UNITAI_RESERVE` roles, and SAM Infantry could also leak into generic attack/reserve selection.
+
+The XML already described the intended role clearly. Both SAM Infantry and Mobile SAM explicitly listed `UNITAI_COUNTER`, `UNITAI_CITY_COUNTER`, and `UNITAI_CITY_SPECIAL`, but their `NotUnitAIs` did not forbid the generic offensive/reserve roles. In `CvPlayerAI::AI_unitValue`, explicit `UnitAIs` are not an exclusive whitelist: if a combat-capable unit is not marked as mostly defensive, generic fallback logic can still consider it valid for `UNITAI_ATTACK`, `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY`, `UNITAI_RESERVE` (and related generic roles) unless `NotUnitAIs` explicitly rejects that role. Mobile SAM's useful combat strength and 2 movement made that fallback particularly capable of competing with ordinary frontline units despite its intended anti-air specialization.
+
+The fix is intentionally data-driven and small: SAM Infantry and Mobile SAM now explicitly add `UNITAI_ATTACK`, `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY`, and `UNITAI_RESERVE` to `NotUnitAIs`. Their intended `UNITAI_COUNTER`, `UNITAI_CITY_COUNTER`, and `UNITAI_CITY_SPECIAL` entries remain unchanged, so the AI can still build and use them as anti-air/counter units. Save-file 459 follow-up BBAI logs showed no selections of either dedicated AA unit into the newly forbidden generic roles.
+
+No blanket percentage cap on land anti-air units is added by this fix. A high anti-air share can still be legitimate if the AI's actual counter logic calls for it, and save file 459 also showed that substantial Mobile-SAM production can occur through `UNITAI_COUNTER`. If legitimate counter-role demand later proves systematically excessive, that can be tuned separately with evidence. This issue specifically fixes specialized AA units consuming ordinary offensive/reserve slots because of generic UnitAI fallback.
+
+Fixed/improved with the help of GPT-5.6 Thinking thanks.

