-- **Reliable Worker availability without early overproduction:** AI cities rebuild enough Workers for each land area before other production priorities can leave them short indefinitely, without producing more than the AI calculates it needs. Maintaining enough Workers throughout the game lets the AI improve food/production/commerce, connect bonuses and routes, repair pillaging, and develop new or underdeveloped cities sooner, especially after Worker losses or earlier underproduction. This normally aims for 1 Worker per city plus a capital-area reserve. As a refinement, a one-city empire needs at most 1 land Worker until its capital has ever reached a tunable population (5 by default), avoiding early double-Worker production and the resulting halted growth; normal demand resumes after the capital matures or another city is founded. Ordinary Worker choices, cases where a blocked Settler choice falls back to a Worker, general unit selection, and Worker scrapping now use the same centralized target. Four BBAI test runs then completed exactly 1 early Worker per empire, with the only later automatic second Worker beginning when its capital reached population 5.
